(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-hematologic-malignancies-ii-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Hematologic Malignancies II Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 22-year-old man in the United States has an abdominal mass that has visibly enlarged over 24 hours. Biopsy shows a starry sky pattern (sheets of lymphoma cells with benign macrophages clearing dying cells), consistent with sporadic Burkitt lymphoma. Which referral is appropriate?","choices":["Repeat imaging in 3 months","Emergent hematology referral","Routine oncology visit in 4 weeks","Surgery to remove the mass first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Burkitt lymphoma's doubling time is extremely fast, with tumor expanding within 24 hours, so treatment must start promptly with emergent referral to a hematologist and rapid transfer of care. Early multi-agent chemotherapy gives an excellent prognosis. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 32, 33"},{"q":"A 71-year-old farmer with years of pesticide exposure has blurred vision and headaches. Serum studies show a large IgM monoclonal protein, and he has signs of hyperviscosity; there are no lytic bone lesions or hypercalcemia. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Multiple myeloma","Smoldering myeloma","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma (Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia) causes excess IgM, a large antibody that causes hyperviscosity with blurred vision and headache. Farming and pesticides are listed risk factors. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 18, 49"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man has biopsy-proven classical Hodgkin lymphoma presenting as a painless cervical node. Which imaging study in the initial work-up checks for a mediastinal mass or adenopathy?","choices":["Upper endoscopy","Skull x-ray","Chest x-ray","Lumbar puncture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A chest x-ray rules out a mediastinal mass or adenopathy, which is common in Hodgkin lymphoma. Work-up also includes a complete blood count, chemistries (electrolytes, for tumor lysis), lactate dehydrogenase and bone marrow biopsy. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 25, 39"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has newly diagnosed Burkitt lymphoma, a high-grade lymphoma. Excisional biopsy, imaging, blood counts, chemistries and lactate dehydrogenase have been done. Which additional procedure is required in his work-up?","choices":["Protein electrophoresis","Lumbar puncture","Fine-needle aspiration","Upper endoscopy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High-grade lymphomas, including Burkitt lymphoma, require a lumbar puncture as part of the work-up, alongside bone marrow biopsy, imaging, a complete blood count, chemistries and lactate dehydrogenase. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has an M-protein of 3.9 g/dL, and bone marrow biopsy shows 24% clonal plasma cells. She has no symptoms; calcium, creatinine and hemoglobin are normal, and whole-body imaging shows no bone lesions. How should her plasma cell disorder be classified?","choices":["Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance","Multiple myeloma","Reactive hypergammaglobulinemia of cirrhosis","Smoldering multiple myeloma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reading the criteria: M-protein above 3 g/dL (3.9) and marrow plasma cells within 10-60% (24%), with no myeloma-defining events (no anemia, bone lesions, renal failure or hypercalcemia). That is smoldering myeloma, an intermediate stage that typically does not require treatment. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 56, 57"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has smoldering multiple myeloma: M-protein 3.6 g/dL and 18% clonal bone marrow plasma cells, with no anemia, bone lesions, renal failure or hypercalcemia. No high-risk subgroup feature is identified. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Allopurinol and fluids","Monitor without treatment","Start a 3-drug regimen now","Autologous stem cell transplant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Smoldering myeloma typically does not require treatment, although early intervention may be considered for some high-risk subgroups; she has none identified. It progresses at about 10% per year, so monitoring continues. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has follicular lymphoma. Positron emission tomography shows involved inguinal lymph nodes and para-aortic lymph nodes in the abdomen; nothing is involved above the diaphragm and no organ is involved. What is her stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two node regions (inguinal and para-aortic), both below the diaphragm, with no organ involvement. Stage 2 is two or more lymph node regions on the same side of the diaphragm, and the same side can be the lower side. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 71-year-old farmer with newly diagnosed chronic lymphocytic leukemia asks whether the pesticides he sprayed for decades caused his leukemia. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Hepatitis C caused it","Radiation likely caused it","No exposure is a known risk","Pesticides likely caused it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia has no known occupational or environmental risk factors. Its risk factors are age (median onset 70), male sex (twice as common), and Eastern European or Jewish descent. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has newly diagnosed multiple myeloma. His beta-2 microglobulin is 6.4 mg/L (normal 0.8-2.2 mg/L) and albumin 3.8 g/dL (normal 3.5-5.0 g/dL); chromosomal studies show only standard-risk abnormalities, and lactate dehydrogenase is normal. What is his R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage III needs all of beta-2 microglobulin at least 5.5 mg/L, high-risk chromosomes and high lactate dehydrogenase; he has only the high beta-2 microglobulin. Stage I needs beta-2 microglobulin below 3.5 mg/L, and his is 6.4. Not stage I or III is stage II. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with interstitial lung disease has newly diagnosed classical Hodgkin lymphoma. The historical regimen of doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine and dacarbazine is being considered. Which drug in it is the concern because of her lungs?","choices":["Bleomycin","Doxorubicin","Vinblastine","Dacarbazine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bleomycin carries pulmonary toxicities, which is why newer agents are replacing it in Hodgkin lymphoma regimens. With pre-existing interstitial lung disease she is the patient in whom bleomycin is most worrying. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has peripheral T-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified, and is to start a CHOP (cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone)-type regimen. Which drug used with the B-cell version of this regimen should be left out?","choices":["Doxorubicin","Prednisone","Rituximab","Cyclophosphamide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. T-cell lymphomas are treated with similar regimens (CHOP (cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone)) minus rituximab, because rituximab is an anti-CD20 (cluster of differentiation 20) antibody and T cells do not express CD20 (cluster of differentiation 20). — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has classical Hodgkin lymphoma. Staging imaging shows involved left cervical lymph nodes and right inguinal lymph nodes, with no liver, bone marrow or other organ involvement. What is his stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cervical nodes are above the diaphragm and inguinal nodes below it; with no organ involvement, two or more node regions above and below the diaphragm is stage 3. His age over 60 is the second peak of Hodgkin lymphoma's bimodal incidence. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man with sporadic Burkitt lymphoma is about to begin multi-agent chemotherapy. His potassium, phosphorus, uric acid and creatinine are currently normal. What should be done before the first dose?","choices":["Give oral potassium supplements","Delay chemotherapy for 2 weeks","Give allopurinol and intravenous fluids","Restrict fluids to protect the kidneys"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tumor lysis syndrome should be anticipated in aggressive lymphoma, and Burkitt lymphoma needs caution when therapy starts. Patients are given allopurinol (a hypouricemic agent) and fluids in advance, with aggressive intravenous hydration. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 28, 33"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. Staging shows enlarged cervical lymph nodes, lymphoma deposits in the liver, and bone marrow biopsy involvement. What is her stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lymphoma in the liver and the bone marrow is widespread disease with multiple organs involved, which is stage 4 with or without lymph node involvement. Her cervical nodes do not change that. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has had painless lymph nodes in the neck and groin that have waxed and waned for 2 years. Excisional biopsy shows follicular lymphoma with a t(14;18) translocation. She has no symptoms. What is the most appropriate management now?","choices":["Emergent hospital admission","Watch and wait","Multi-drug chemotherapy now","Allogeneic stem cell transplant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Follicular lymphoma, the most common indolent non-Hodgkin lymphoma, frequently needs no treatment (watch and wait); intermittent low-intensity therapy is used if symptomatic. She is asymptomatic, so observation fits. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 16, 27, 34"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has an absolute lymphocyte count of 4,600 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) on routine blood work. Flow cytometry shows a clonal B-cell population. She has no symptoms, no lymphadenopathy and no hepatomegaly or splenomegaly; hemoglobin and platelets are normal. How should this finding be classified?","choices":["Monoclonal B lymphocytosis","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Infectious atypical lymphocytosis","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her absolute lymphocyte count is elevated but below 5,000 per microliter, and she has no lymphadenopathy, organomegaly, cytopenias or symptoms. That is monoclonal B lymphocytosis, which progresses to chronic lymphocytic leukemia at about 1-2% per year. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 8, 9"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has newly diagnosed symptomatic multiple myeloma. Which initial regimen is used?","choices":["Rituximab with cyclophosphamide and doxorubicin","Doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine and dacarbazine","Venetoclax with obinutuzumab for a fixed duration","Proteasome inhibitor, immunomodulatory drug and steroid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Newly diagnosed patients are treated with a 3- or 4-drug regimen with a proteasome inhibitor and immunomodulatory drug backbone with steroids. The goal is prolonged remission to improve survival and quality of life, as myeloma is incurable. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has an absolute lymphocyte count of 25,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) for 4 months and no symptoms. Flow cytometry is sent on his peripheral blood. Which result supports chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","choices":["A clonal B-cell population","Binucleated Reed-Sternberg cells","Polyclonal reactive T cells","Clonal plasma cells making IgG"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Flow cytometry confirms chronic lymphocytic leukemia by identifying a clonal B-cell population with coexpression of CD19 (cluster of differentiation 19) and CD5 (cluster of differentiation 5) on the lymphocytes. With his count above 5,000 for 3 months, that confirms it. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman has just been told she has chronic lymphocytic leukemia, Rai stage 0. She asks what her long-term outlook is. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["She is likely to die with it, not of it","She is likely to die of it in 2 years","It is cured by a short chemotherapy course","It turns into aggressive lymphoma in a year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Five-year relative survival is about 88%, much improved by novel agents, and the elderly age at onset with ongoing new therapies means many patients will die with chronic lymphocytic leukemia rather than from it. Long-term prognosis is likely very good. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman's excisional lymph node biopsy shows diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. Which study is required next to characterize and stage her disease?","choices":["Fine-needle aspiration of each remaining node","Serum protein electrophoresis for monoclonal protein","Computed tomography or positron emission tomography","Physical examination and blood counts alone for staging"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Imaging, usually computed tomography or positron emission tomography, is required to characterize and appropriately stage lymphoma by the Lugano classification. Work-up also includes a complete blood count, chemistries, lactate dehydrogenase and bone marrow biopsy. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 10, 25, 26, 50"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man was treated with chemotherapy for Hodgkin lymphoma at age 25 and has been in remission since. He now has new cytopenias, and marrow evaluation shows a myelodysplastic syndrome. How is this best explained?","choices":["A relapse of Hodgkin lymphoma","Richter transformation","His inherited sibling risk","A delayed toxicity of his therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma is generally curable (more than 90% alive at 5 years), but survivors may have delayed toxicities, including myelodysplastic syndrome, secondary leukemia and other cancers. Two decades after chemotherapy, his new marrow disease fits. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with aggressive lymphoma develops tumor lysis syndrome on day 2 of chemotherapy, with rising potassium, phosphorus, uric acid and creatinine. Which consequence of this syndrome carries the largest risk?","choices":["Hyperkalemic arrhythmia","Acute renal failure","Hypercalcemic constipation","Hyperviscosity stroke"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The largest risk of tumor lysis syndrome is hyperkalemia leading to arrhythmia and potential death. Acute renal failure from uric acid and calcium phosphate crystals also occurs and worsens the condition, but the potassium is the killer. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has painless, firm, mobile lymph nodes in the neck and both axillae. His absolute lymphocyte count is 4,500 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000), and flow cytometry shows a clonal B-cell population coexpressing CD19 (cluster of differentiation 19) and CD5 (cluster of differentiation 5). How should this be classified?","choices":["Monoclonal B lymphocytosis","Infectious atypical lymphocytosis","Classical Hodgkin lymphoma","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is diagnosed with a count above 5,000 for 3 months or a count below 5,000 with lymphadenopathy or other symptoms. His count is 4,500, but he has lymphadenopathy, and the clone coexpresses CD19 (cluster of differentiation 19) and CD5 (cluster of differentiation 5). — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 8, 9"},{"q":"A 75-year-old woman with chronic lymphocytic leukemia has been observed for 2 years. She has no symptoms, but her absolute lymphocyte count has risen from 18,000 to 41,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) over 4 months. What is the next step?","choices":["Continue watch and wait","Repeat the count in a year","Obtain a bone marrow biopsy","Begin targeted therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Observation lasts until rapid progression or disease-related symptoms. Her count has more than doubled in 4 months, under the 6-month mark, which is rapid progression and an indication to treat, with targeted therapies such as a Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitor. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 8, 11"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has diffuse large B-cell lymphoma with involved axillary and inguinal lymph nodes and no extranodal sites. He spends most of the day in a chair, with an ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) performance status of 3, and his lactate dehydrogenase is elevated. How many poor-prognosis factors does he have?","choices":["One factor","Two factors","Three factors","Four factors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Item by item: age over 60, yes (72); extranodal sites, no; ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) status above 2, yes (3); stage III or IV, yes (axillary nodes above and inguinal nodes below the diaphragm, stage III); elevated lactate dehydrogenase, yes. Total 4. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 26, 30"},{"q":"A 72-year-old Black man has 2 months of back and pelvic pain, constipation, increased thirst, urinary frequency, fatigue and ankle swelling. Calcium is 11.9 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5 mg/dL), hemoglobin 9.6 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL) and creatinine 1.9 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.2 mg/dL); skull x-ray shows multiple punched-out lytic lesions. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hodgkin lymphoma","Multiple myeloma","Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His findings are the CRAB (calcium, renal, anemia, bone) features: hypercalcemia (constipation, thirst, urinary frequency), renal insufficiency (swelling), anemia (fatigue) and bone pain with punched-out osteolytic lesions. Age over 65 and African American race raise myeloma risk. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 46, 48"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has newly diagnosed multiple myeloma. His beta-2 microglobulin is 2.9 mg/L (normal 0.8-2.2 mg/L), albumin 4.0 g/dL (normal 3.5-5.0 g/dL), chromosomal studies show only standard-risk abnormalities, and lactate dehydrogenase is normal. What is his R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage I requires every item: beta-2 microglobulin below 3.5 mg/L (2.9), albumin at least 3.5 g/dL (4.0), standard-risk chromosomal abnormalities, and normal lactate dehydrogenase. He meets all four. The stage is prognostic and does not broadly change treatment. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with chronic lymphocytic leukemia now needs treatment for symptomatic disease. She prefers a time-limited course rather than taking pills indefinitely, even if it is more intensive. Which approach fits her preference?","choices":["Venetoclax with obinutuzumab","Ibrutinib monotherapy","Acalabrutinib monotherapy","Observation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A BCL-2 (B-cell lymphoma 2) inhibitor, venetoclax, is combined with a monoclonal antibody such as obinutuzumab for a fixed duration (infusion plus pill, time limited but more intensive). Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitors are usually continuous as monotherapy. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 11, 13"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has diffuse large B-cell lymphoma with involved lymph nodes in the neck and the groin and no extranodal sites. His ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) performance status is 1, and his lactate dehydrogenase is above the upper limit of normal. How many poor-prognosis factors does he have?","choices":["One factor","Two factors","Three factors","Four factors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Item by item from the poor-prognosis list: age over 60, yes (67); extranodal sites, no; ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) status above 2, no (1); stage III or IV, yes (neck and groin nodes span the diaphragm, stage III); elevated lactate dehydrogenase, yes. Total 3. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 26, 30"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has an absolute lymphocyte count of 14,500 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) that has persisted for 4 months, and flow cytometry confirms chronic lymphocytic leukemia. He has no symptoms, no palpable lymph nodes and no hepatomegaly or splenomegaly. Hemoglobin is 14.6 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL) and platelets 228,000 per microliter (normal 150,000-450,000). What is his Rai stage?","choices":["Stage 0","Stage I","Stage II","Stage III"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Reading the Rai table item by item: lymphocytosis yes (14,500); lymphadenopathy no; organomegaly (hepatomegaly or splenomegaly) no; anemia no (14.6 g/dL); thrombocytopenia no (228,000). Asymptomatic lymphocytosis alone is stage 0, staged from the exam and labs without advanced imaging. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
